FYI Personally I'd do:
```cpp
auto& baseColors = *reinterpret_cast<std::array<Color, 16>*>(&s.Black);
for (size_t i = 0; i < 16; ++i)
{
    baseColors[i] = til::color{ _renderSettings.GetColorTableEntry(i) };
}
```

and
```cpp
const auto& baseColors = *reinterpret_cast<const std::array<Color, 16>*>(&colorScheme.Black);
for (size_t i = 0; i < 16; ++i)
{
    _renderSettings.SetColorTableEntry(i, til::color{ baseColors[i] });
}
```

I mean... It's not like it's our fault that MIDL3 can't do arrays. It practically forced our hand here! We had to reinterpret cast it in order to survive another day in this world! Also code doesn't break code, humans break code. So if we're just careful to not break the code... this reinterpret cast works forever. /s
(In all seriousness though, I'd personally use reinterpret cast here, because it makes the code vastly shorter.)
